{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"gorgeable-debonair-cuckoo","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# Replace the shape heuristic with registry membership, the mechanism every comparable project uses\n\n## Why this exists\n\nParent ticket `03b0` fixed a real bug — a canonical Bedrock model id\n(`anthropic.claude-haiku-4-5-20251001-v1:0`) contains a colon in its version suffix, so a\n`\":\" in model` test read it as \"already provider-qualified\" and SILENTLY DISCARDED the operator's\nconfigured `provider = \"bedrock\"`. That fix shipped with a SHAPE test: a prefix counts as a provider\nonly if it matches `^[a-z][a-z0-9_-]*$` (identifier-like, crucially no DOTS, since Bedrock ids are\ndotted).\n\nThe shape test works and is tested, but a survey of comparable projects found it has **no precedent**,\nand the reason given for choosing it does not survive contact with how those projects are built.\n\n## What the survey established (source-verified, with citations on `03b0`)\n\n**Registry membership is the norm; nobody uses a shape rule:**\n- **LangChain** `init_chat_model`: `model.split(\":\", 1)[0] in _BUILTIN_PROVIDERS`\n- **LiteLLM**: `model.split(\"/\", 1)[0] in litellm.provider_list`\n- **pydantic-ai**: `parse_model_id` + `infer_provider_class`, which raises `Unknown provider: …`\n- **Vercel AI SDK**: registry lookup, `NoSuchProviderError` listing `availableProviders`\n- **simonw/llm**: flat exact-match registry, `UnknownModelError`\n\n**The objection that blocked the registry is answerable, and two projects show exactly how.** The\nstated reason for the shape test was that `_resolve_target` runs during config resolution, where the\nlive registry needs either an `LLMConfig` instance (`ProviderSession._builders`) or an import of the\noptional `[agents]` extra (`_pydantic_ai_known_providers()` imports `pydantic_ai`). But:\n- LangChain's `_BUILTIN_PROVIDERS` is a module-level dict of provider name -> LAZY IMPORT\n  COORDINATES (`\"bedrock\": (\"langchain_aws\", \"ChatBedrock\", …)`). Consulting the NAME SET imports\n  nothing; the provider module is imported later, only when something is built.\n- LiteLLM's `provider_list` is explicitly lazy-loaded, with the in-repo comment\n  \"provider_list is lazy-loaded via __getattr__ to avoid importing LlmProviders at import time\" —\n  the same dependency-light constraint, already solved.\n\nThe design move both made is to **separate \"which names are providers\" (a static set of strings)\nfrom \"what implements them\" (resolved downstream)**. rebar can do the same.\n\n## What the shape test cannot do, which is the concrete motivation\n\nA typo'd provider passes silently and produces a confusing downstream error instead of a diagnostic:\n\n    _resolve_target(\"claude-opus-4-8\", \"bedrok\")  ->  'bedrok:claude-opus-4-8'\n\nA registry raises `unknown provider 'bedrok'; valid: [...]` at the point of the mistake. This is the\nsame failure SHAPE as the bug `03b0` fixed — a configuration error that surfaces late, wearing an\noutage's clothes — so leaving it is leaving half the lesson unlearned. Secondary: any future model id\nwhose prefix happens to be a lowercase dotless identifier before a colon would silently become a\n\"provider\". Bedrock ids are dotted today, but that is AWS convention, not a contract.\n\n## Explicitly NOT in scope, because `03b0` already did it\n\nThe **guard order** — an explicitly configured provider is checked FIRST, before the model string is\nscanned at all, so no punctuation can overrule it (LangChain's `not model_provider and …` condition).\nThat is the more important half of the bug class and it already landed in `03b0`, along with raising\non a genuinely conflicting qualifier. This ticket changes only HOW an unconfigured string is tested\nfor qualification.\n\nAlso out of scope: **changing the separator**. `:` stays. pydantic-ai's own `KnownModelName` contains\ntwo-colon entries (`'bedrock:anthropic.claude-haiku-4-5-20251001-v1:0'`), so \"provider, first colon,\nthen a model name that may itself contain colons\" IS our runtime's grammar. `/` (LiteLLM, OpenRouter)\nis collision-free for Bedrock and arguably better, but no project was found regretting or migrating a\nseparator, and switching would be a breaking config change for no correctness gain.\n\n## THE REGRESSION RISK, which is the main thing the plan must address\n\nSwitching from shape to membership makes the test STRICTER, so a provider name that is\nidentifier-shaped and currently works, but is absent from our static set, would newly be treated as\nUNQUALIFIED and get double-prefixed or rejected. pydantic-ai recognizes many providers rebar does not\nbuild (`google_genai`, `mistral`, `groq`, `cohere`, …). The static set must therefore cover every\nname that resolves today, not just rebar's own three builders (`anthropic`, `bedrock`, and `openai`\nwhen `base_url` is set). Getting this wrong breaks working configurations, which matters because this\nproject dogfoods itself.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] the provider-name set is a static, module-level collection of STRINGS in the config layer,\n      importing nothing optional — `import rebar.llm` must stay stdlib-only, asserted by a test\n- [ ] the qualifier decision uses membership in that set, and the shape regex is removed rather than\n      left as a second, divergent answer to the same question\n- [ ] a provider prefix that is NOT in the set produces a clear error naming the invalid provider and\n      listing the valid ones — the capability the shape test cannot provide. Asserted with the\n      `bedrok` case specifically\n- [ ] BACK-COMPAT, enumerated and tested: every provider name that resolves today still resolves.\n      Derive the list from `ProviderSession._builders` plus `_pydantic_ai_known_providers()` and pin\n      it in a test, so an omission fails loudly here rather than silently in a user's config\n- [ ] the static set and the runtime builder registry cannot drift: either single-source them, or add\n      a test asserting every runtime-registered provider name appears in the static set\n- [ ] both deciders (`_resolve_target` and `infer_provider`) continue to route through the ONE shared\n      function `split_provider_qualifier` — the original defect was two independent implementations of\n      one question, and that must not return\n- [ ] both canonical Bedrock id shapes (versioned `...-v1:0` and unversioned alias) still resolve\n      correctly with a configured provider — the `03b0` regression tests must keep passing unchanged\n\n## A third option, recorded because our own runtime chose it\n\npydantic-ai REMOVED bare-name inference in v2.0.0b1: `Agent('gpt-5')` now raises a `UserError` and you\nmust write `openai:gpt-5`. If rebar required explicit qualification, this whole ambiguity would not\nexist. That is the cleanest end state and the natural direction of travel, but it is a breaking config\nchange and a separate decision — noted here so it is not rediscovered later.","id":"7b22-d19f-2600-4c42","parent_id":"03b0-2290-f4f3-47a8","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Decide the provider qualifier by registry membership, not prefix shape, matching OSS consensus"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785628947267256001,"uuid":"828b082c-072f-477f-a25c-6d5b5c8dbb04"}